  <h2>Why is it hard to compare QMS software prices directly?</h2>
  <p>
    Vendors price by different units: per named user, per concurrent user, per module, or by a flat tier.
    So a "$X/month" headline from one vendor rarely means the same thing as another's. Work out your real
    number of editors, viewers and the modules you need before comparing quotes, or you'll compare
    figures that describe differently sized deployments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Per-user pricing (Effivity, Isolocity) rewards a small team and punishes growth: add ten users and the
    bill scales with them. Tiered pricing (Obel MS, and the entry level of most enterprise platforms) caps
    what you pay at a set number of editors, which is easier to budget against but can force an early
    upgrade if you cross the line. Many quality managers only start comparing prices seriously in the weeks
    before an audit. That is the wrong time to discover that a "starting from" figure excludes
    implementation, training and a six-figure onboarding project. Ask for the fully loaded number as early
    as you can.
  </p>

  <h2>What drives the cost of QMS software?</h2>
  <p>
    Seven factors move the price more than anything else: how many people need to log in, which modules
    you switch on, where the software runs, whether it needs formal validation, how much setup work your
    data needs, the migration effort from spreadsheets or another system, and what level of support
    you expect. Ask a vendor to price against your real numbers on each one.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Users and seats.</strong> Named-editor pricing (Effivity, Isolocity) scales with headcount. Tiered pricing caps the editor count per plan and often includes unlimited read-only viewers instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Modules.</strong> Document control alone costs less than document control plus CAPA, nonconformance, change control, audit and document review workflows bundled together.</li>
    <li><strong>Hosting vs on-premise.</strong> A hosted (SaaS) system folds infrastructure, patching and uptime into the subscription. An on-premise or self-hosted system shifts that cost, and the IT effort, onto you.</li>
    <li><strong>Validation.</strong> Life-science and medical-device buyers often need IQ/OQ/PQ-style validation documentation. Some vendors price it in (SimplerQMS), others charge it as a separate project (Qualio's implementation fee).</li>
    <li><strong>Implementation.</strong> Configuring templates, roles, approval routes and folder structures to match how your business works.</li>
    <li><strong>Migration.</strong> Moving existing registers, spreadsheets and document history in without losing version history or audit evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Training and support.</strong> Self-serve tools bundle basic training. Enterprise vendors often charge separately, or gate faster support behind a higher tier.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What hidden costs should you budget for?</h2>
  <p>
    The listed price rarely is the final price. Enterprise eQMS vendors in particular treat implementation,
    validation and training as separate line items rather than folding them into the subscription. That's
    how a "$12,000/year" headline turns into tens of thousands more before you've logged your first
    document. Ask about the items below before you sign. They are the ones most likely to turn up on an
    invoice you didn't expect.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Hidden cost</th>
          <th>What to ask</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Setup or onboarding fee</td><td>Is it once-off, and is it waived on an annual commitment?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Storage overages</td><td>What happens once you exceed the included storage, and what does extra storage cost per month?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per-module add-ons</td><td>Which modules are included in the headline price, and which are priced separately?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI or automation usage caps</td><td>Is there a monthly limit on AI-processed documents or automated steps, and what does going over cost?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Support tier upsells</td><td>Is email-only support standard, with faster response times reserved for a higher plan?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Data migration</td><td>Is importing your existing registers and spreadsheets included, or a separate line item?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exit and export</td><td>Can you export your full document set, metadata and audit trail on request, at no extra cost, if you leave?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Currency and VAT</td><td>Is the quoted price VAT-inclusive, and in which currency will you be billed?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

